**Assistant** [1]

**Pokémon canon never firmly says there is only one Mew.**

### Games

Mew is treated as an extraordinarily rare **species**, supposedly containing the genetic information of every Pokémon. The games usually present only one encounter, but that is gameplay and does not establish a population count.

The “ancestor of Pokémon” idea also implies that numerous Mew may once have existed, even if only one or a few survive today.

### Anime

The anime has presented Mew in several distinct contexts:

- the Mew connected to Mewtwo in the first movie,
- the Mew living around the Tree of Beginning in *Lucario and the Mystery of Mew*, [official synopsis](https://www.pokemon.com/us/animation/movies/pokemon-lucario-and-the-mystery-of-mew),
- and the Mew pursued by Project Mew in *Pokémon Journeys*, [official episode synopsis](https://www.pokemon.com/us/animation/seasons/25/episode-39-the-mew-from-here).

Nothing authoritative clearly establishes that all of these are the same individual. The anime therefore permits multiple Mew, though it rarely says so explicitly.

## For *Ambertwo*

Any of these models would be canon-compatible:

1. **Mew was once a population; several individuals still survive.**
2. **Mew was once a population, but only one remains in the modern world.**
3. **One Mew exists, but it travels globally and appears in different legends.**
4. **“Mew” is a higher-order being capable of producing multiple physical manifestations.**

If Argaea has its own Mew guardian, the first model is the simplest: Mew are an almost-extinct ancestral species, and one individual became trapped with Argaea while another supplied the genetic material eventually used for Mewtwo.

That avoids making the Argaean Mew, the Tree of Beginning Mew, and Fuji’s Mew all the same overworked individual.
### Darkrai: yes, clearly multiple individuals exist

In the anime continuity, we have:

- the Darkrai protecting Alamos Town in *The Rise of Darkrai*,
- the wild Darkrai that visits Canalave City,
- Tobias’s trained Darkrai,
- and a Darkrai appearing with Cresselia much later in *Pokémon Journeys*.

These cannot all reasonably be the same individual. The Canalave episode even describes a Darkrai that visits annually and a local Cresselia that drives it away. [Official episode synopsis](https://www.pokemon.com/uk/animation/seasons/11/episode-52-sleepless-in-pre-battle)

### Cresselia: probably multiple, though less explicitly demonstrated

Canon never identifies Cresselia as a unique deity. It is treated as a very rare species:

- one inhabits or visits Fullmoon Island,
- another—or possibly the same one—appears injured during *Pokémon Journeys* alongside a Darkrai. [Official episode synopsis](https://www.pokemon.com/uk/animation/seasons/24/episode-27-a-midsummer-nights-light)

There is no firm statement that every Cresselia appearance is one individual.

## Their relationship

Cresselia and Darkrai are better understood as **counterpart species**, not necessarily two unique cosmic beings:

- Darkrai produces nightmares.
- Cresselia dispels nightmares and promotes peaceful sleep.
- Individual Darkrai and Cresselia can develop local relationships, rivalries, or balances.

Therefore, Argaea can plausibly possess **its own ancient Cresselia and Darkrai** without consuming the Darkrai from Alamos, Tobias’s Darkrai, or the Cresselia associated with Fullmoon Island.

That is cleaner than using Mew. An Argaean Cresselia–Darkrai pair does not force every Mewtwo and Mew storyline to connect to the kingdom.

